insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-an-assay-carries', 1, 'The studio answers four questions, each with its own function. Which function answers whether a mixture will drop asphaltenes in the tank?', '["colloidalInstabilityIndex, run on each crude alone.", "screenBlendStability, which runs inside blendCrudes.", "blendViscosity, since an asphaltene sludge first shows itself as a thicker blend.", "watsonK, which characterises each crude."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The function table pairs the tank question with screenBlendStability (inside blendCrudes). colloidalInstabilityIndex is printed for each crude alone, and neither blendViscosity nor watsonK answers any of the four questions.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-an-assay-carries', 2, 'The module table counts what each engine module exports. What does crudeAssay export?', '["5 functions and 4 constants, among them SPEC_TEMPLATES and BLEND_BASIS.", "1 function, solveLP, and 1 constant, LP_STATUS.", "18 functions and 4 constants, among them BINDING_TOLERANCE.", "18 functions and 1 table, CII_BANDS."]'::jsonb, 3, 'crudeAssay exports 18 functions and 1 table, CII_BANDS. The 5 functions and 4 constants are productBlending''s row, and solveLP with LP_STATUS is lib/lp/simplex.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-an-assay-carries', 3, 'Which function does productBlending call in lib/lp/simplex, and where do its gravity and viscosity rules come from?', '["solveLP, with the gravity and viscosity rules imported from crudeAssay.", "solveLP, with its own restated copy of the gravity and viscosity rules.", "optimiseBlend, with the sulfur and SARA rules imported from crudeAssay.", "blendCrudes, with the gravity and viscosity rules taken from lib/lp/simplex."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Product Blending Optimizer calls productBlending, which calls the LP kernel solveLP in lib/lp/simplex and imports the gravity and viscosity rules from crudeAssay rather than restating them. optimiseBlend is productBlending''s own function, and blendCrudes is crudeAssay''s.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-an-assay-carries', 4, 'sgFromApi is SG = A / (API + B). What values does the engine give for A and B when they are read back from apiFromSg?', '["A is 131.5000 and B is 141.5000.", "A is 14.5340 and B is 10.9750.", "A is 141.5000 and B is 131.5000.", "A is 141.5000 and B is 10.0000, the API of water."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A is apiFromSg(0.5) minus apiFromSg(1), 141.5000, and B is A minus apiFromSg(1), 131.5000. 14.5340 and 10.9750 are the Refutas constants, and 10.0000 is water''s API.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-an-assay-carries', 5, 'The engine is handed specific gravity in equal steps from 0.75 to 1. What does the API step column print?', '["One step repeated on every row, -9.2484, because API is a straight line in specific gravity.", "Steps that grow toward water, from -7.4474 near 0.75 to -11.7917 at 1.", "A different step on every row, from -11.7917 on the first step to -7.4474 on the last.", "A step of -11.7917 at the light end, then one constant step for the rest of the way to water."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The table prints five different steps: -11.7917, -10.4044, -9.2484, -8.2748 and -7.4474. API is a hyperbola in specific gravity: equal steps of specific gravity are unequal steps of API.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-an-assay-carries', 6, 'Each library API goes into sgFromApi and comes back through apiFromSg to four decimals: 17.2 returns 17.2000, 54.6 returns 54.6000. What does the round trip show?', '["apiFromSg is the inverse of sgFromApi: each API comes back as it went in.", "Each API comes back moved a little, and the round trip column prints the drift.", "API blends on a straight line, because a round trip through specific gravity is linear.", "The library''s API figures were themselves computed from measured specific gravities."]'::jsonb, 0, 'sgFromApi is SG = A / (API + B), and apiFromSg is its inverse. Every row of the round trip column returns the API it was given: 10.0000, 17.2000, 54.6000 and the rest. In the library, API is the input and specific gravity is the computed column.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-an-assay-carries', 7, 'Which column of the library''s property table does the engine compute instead of taking it as typed?', '["The API, from a measured specific gravity through apiFromSg.", "The viscosity, from each crude''s Refutas index through viscosityFromBlendIndex.", "The sulfur, from the saturates and aromatics in the SARA analysis.", "The specific gravity, from API through sgFromApi."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Specific gravity is computed from API by sgFromApi, so it prints to four decimals (Obigbo Light 0.8408). API, sulfur, viscosity and SARA are inputs and print exactly as typed.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-an-assay-carries', 8, 'What does the Ebocha partial assay carry beside its curve?', '["A specific gravity of 0.8686 typed from a laboratory, with its API computed from it.", "An API of 31.4 and a sulfur of 0.22 wt%, and nothing else.", "An API, a sulfur and a SARA analysis, with only the metals and viscosity not given.", "A full property row, with the blanks typed as 0."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Ebocha row carries API 31.4 and sulfur 0.22; every other property reads not given. Its 0.8686 is computed from the API, and a not given field is a blank. A blank is no zero.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-an-assay-carries', 9, 'Which crude in the library has a curve that cannot say what happens at 100 percent distilled?', '["Ebocha partial assay, whose last point is 88 at 920.", "Asarama Heavy, whose curve ends at 1560.", "Ubie Condensate, whose curve ends at 760 F.", "Obigbo Light, because the gap from 90 at 1060 to its end point is unmeasured."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Four curves run from 0 to 100 percent. The Ebocha curve starts at 4 percent and stops at 88, and outside a curve''s range the value is unknown except where the curve itself says so, below a first point at 0 percent or above a last point at 100.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-an-assay-carries', 10, 'Which library crude carries the most vanadium, and how much?', '["Asarama Heavy, at 41 ppm, the figure in its metals column.", "Egbema Medium, at 8.7 ppm.", "Obigbo Light, at 1.4 ppm.", "Asarama Heavy, at 96 ppm."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Asarama Heavy''s vanadium is 96 ppm, the largest in the vanadium column (8.7, 1.4 and 0.2 for the others, Ebocha not given). Its 41 ppm is nickel.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-an-assay-carries', 11, 'Which library crude has the highest API and the lowest specific gravity?', '["Obigbo Light, at 36.8 API and 0.8408, the light crude of the library.", "Ubie Condensate, at 54.6 API and 0.7603.", "Asarama Heavy, at 17.2 API and 0.9516.", "Ebocha partial assay, at 31.4 API and 0.8686."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Ubie Condensate''s 54.6 API and 0.7603 are the highest API and lowest specific gravity in the library. Water, at specific gravity 1, is 10.0000 API.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-an-assay-carries', 12, 'Which of the two apps chooses the shares of a blend?', '["The Crude Assay & Blending Studio.", "Neither app; both take the shares a user types.", "The Product Blending Optimizer, which returns a least-cost recipe.", "The studio, through screenBlendStability."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The optimizer''s one question is the least-cost recipe that meets every specification, answered by optimiseBlend. None of the studio''s four questions asks for a recipe.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-an-assay-carries', 13, 'Library inputs such as 4.6 cSt and 22 cSt print with one decimal or none, while specific gravity prints as 0.8408. Why?', '["Inputs print as typed, and every computed figure prints to four decimals.", "The engine rounds each input to its measured precision.", "Viscosity is stored in whole cSt, and specific gravity needs four decimals for API.", "Only specific gravity is blended, so only it carries full precision."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The digest''s precision rule: every computed figure prints to four decimals, and every input prints exactly as it is typed. Viscosity in the library is typed, and specific gravity is computed from API by sgFromApi.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-an-assay-carries', 14, 'Why can a quantity that mixes on a straight line in specific gravity not also mix on a straight line in API?', '["API is quoted at a reference temperature that differs from the one used for specific gravity.", "API is defined per unit mass, while specific gravity is defined per unit volume.", "API is a hyperbola in specific gravity, so equal steps of one are unequal steps of the other.", "API is typed with one decimal and specific gravity with four, so the averages diverge."]'::jsonb, 2, 'API = A / SG - B is a hyperbola in specific gravity: equal steps of specific gravity are unequal steps of API. The engine blends specific gravity on volume and converts the blended figure back to API; API itself is never averaged.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-an-assay-carries', 15, 'The Ebocha partial assay''s TAN reads not given. How does the engine treat it when a blend needs TAN?', '["As 0, so Ebocha dilutes the blend''s TAN.", "As absent: it names Ebocha and returns TAN as not blended.", "As the mean TAN of the four full crudes.", "As grounds to refuse the whole blend."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The library keeps blanks as blanks. The engine never reads not given as a zero; it names the crude that lacks the value and returns the property as not blended, while the other properties still blend.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-blending-on-the-right-basis', 1, 'Obigbo Light and Egbema Medium are blended 65 and 35 by volume. What API does blendCrudes return, and how is it formed?', '["32.9850, the mean of the two API numbers weighted by barrels.", "32.8173, the mean of the two API numbers weighted by barrels.", "32.8173, converted from the blend''s specific gravity of 0.8611.", "0.8611, since the engine reports the blend''s gravity in specific gravity only."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine blends specific gravity on volume, 0.8611, and converts it to 32.8173 API. 32.9850 is the volume-weighted mean of the API numbers, the shortcut the engine refuses; blend API minus that mean is -0.1677.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-blending-on-the-right-basis', 2, 'Half a 20 API crude and half a 40 API crude are blended by volume. What does the engine give, and what does averaging the API numbers by volume give?', '["The engine gives 29.3808; the volume average of the API numbers gives 30.0000.", "The engine gives 30.0000; the mass-weighted mean of the API numbers gives 29.3808.", "The engine gives 29.3808 by averaging the API numbers by volume.", "The engine gives 30.0000, then adds -0.6192 to it as a correction for the hyperbola."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine blends the two specific gravities (0.8795) and converts back, 29.3808. The shortcut says 30.0000, and blend API minus that mean prints -0.6192. The engine applies no correction: it never averages API at all.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-blending-on-the-right-basis', 3, 'Which weighted mean of the API numbers agrees with the engine''s blend API on every row of the gravity table?', '["The volume-weighted mean, as specific gravity blends on volume.", "Neither: no weighting of API numbers reproduces it.", "The volume-weighted mean once -0.6192 is subtracted from it on every row.", "The mass-weighted mean, with a difference of 0.0000 on every row."]'::jsonb, 3, 'API = A / SG - B, and 1 / SG blends linearly on mass, so the mass-weighted mean of the API numbers is the blend API; the difference column prints 0.0000 on all four rows. The -0.6192 belongs to the 20 and 40 API blend alone.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-blending-on-the-right-basis', 4, 'The mass-weighted mean of the API numbers matches the blend API to four decimals. How does the engine itself form the blend API?', '["It forms the mass-weighted mean of the API numbers, which the difference column confirms.", "It blends specific gravity on volume and converts the result to API.", "It averages API by volume and then applies the blend API minus that mean as a correction.", "It blends the API numbers on mass fractions it has formed from the typed volume shares."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine blends specific gravity on volume and converts it back to API; API itself is never averaged. Its basis for API reads: computed from the volume-blended specific gravity, never averaged directly. The mass-weighted mean is printed beside it, where the difference column reads 0.0000.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-blending-on-the-right-basis', 5, 'In the export blend Obigbo Light is 0.6500 of the volume. What is its mass fraction, and why does it move that way?', '["0.6500: a share typed by volume is carried over to the mass basis unchanged.", "0.6346: at 0.8408 it is lighter than the blend, so it carries less of the mass.", "0.3654: the lighter crude takes the smaller mass share once its density is applied.", "0.6346: at 0.8408 it is denser than the blend, so its share shrinks on the mass basis."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Mass share is volume share times specific gravity over the sum of those products. Obigbo Light''s mass fraction minus volume fraction prints -0.0154, and Egbema Medium, at 0.8990, gains the same 0.0154 to reach 0.3654.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-blending-on-the-right-basis', 6, 'In the three-crude blend at 50, 30 and 20 by volume, Egbema Medium''s mass fraction minus volume fraction is 0.0063. Why is it positive, when Asarama Heavy is denser still?', '["It is listed second, and the engine shares the mass gain out in listing order.", "Asarama Heavy''s gain is split with it, because the fractions must add to one.", "Its sulfur is higher than Obigbo Light''s, and the mass shares follow the sulfur.", "Its 0.8990 is denser than the blend''s 0.8804, and the sign is decided against the blend."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Which way a share moves is decided against the blend as a whole. Obigbo Light at 0.8408 prints -0.0225, Egbema Medium at 0.8990 prints 0.0063 and Asarama Heavy at 0.9516 prints 0.0162, all beside a blend of 0.8804.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-blending-on-the-right-basis', 7, 'The same numbers 50, 30 and 20 are typed as MASS shares for Obigbo Light, Egbema Medium and Asarama Heavy. What API does the engine give?', '["29.6100, a different blend from the 29.2240 typed by volume.", "29.2240, since a share''s basis does not change the blend.", "A refusal: a mass share needs a volume share beside it.", "29.2240 on volume, beside a mass-basis API of 29.6100."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Typed by mass, the three crudes make a blend of specific gravity 0.8783 and API 29.6100; typed by volume, 0.8804 and 29.2240. blendCrudes takes every crude by volume or every crude by mass, and the two give different blends.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-blending-on-the-right-basis', 8, 'One crude in a recipe is given a volume share and the other a mass share. What does blendCrudes do?', '["It converts the mass share to volume through that crude''s specific gravity and blends.", "It blends the crude with the volume share and names the other crude as missing.", "It refuses: every crude a volume share, or every crude a mass share.", "It treats both figures as volume shares and names the blend''s basis as mixed."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The refusal reads: Give every crude a volume share, or give every crude a mass share. The two cannot be mixed. Nothing is converted and nothing is blended.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-blending-on-the-right-basis', 9, 'What sulfur does blendCrudes give the Obigbo export blend, and on what basis?', '["0.2642 wt%, on mass.", "0.2590 wt%, on volume, the fraction a cargo is measured in.", "0.2642 wt%, on volume fraction, the basis of the typed shares.", "0.0052 wt%, the part of the sulfur the mass basis adds."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Sulfur is per unit mass, so the engine weights it by mass fraction and names the basis mass: 0.2642. 0.2590 is the same property on volume, printed only to be read against the answer; mass minus volume is 0.0052.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-blending-on-the-right-basis', 10, 'Nickel in the three-crude blend: which pair of figures is the engine''s and the volume shortcut''s?', '["13.5500 ppm on mass, and 14.2202 ppm on volume.", "14.2202 ppm on mass, and 0.6702 ppm on volume.", "14.2202 ppm on mass, and 13.5500 ppm on volume.", "6.5980 ppm on mass, and 6.4550 ppm on volume."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The per-mass table''s nickel row for the three crudes reads 14.2202 on mass, 13.5500 on volume and 0.6702 for the gap. The export blend''s nickel rows are 6.5980 and 6.4550.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-blending-on-the-right-basis', 11, 'The export blend is typed as 13 and 7 instead of 65 and 35. What does the engine return?', '["A refusal, because the shares do not add to 100.", "The same API, with the sulfur rescaled to the smaller total.", "The same API, with sulfur not blended until the shares reach 100.", "The same blend, since the shares are normalised before blending."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The shares are normalised: 65 and 35, 13 and 7, and 650000 and 350000 all return 32.8173 and 0.2642. A negative share and shares that add up to zero are refused.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-blending-on-the-right-basis', 12, 'Egbema Medium''s sulfur is left blank in the export blend. What does blendCrudes return for the blend''s sulfur?', '["0.0888 wt%, with the blank read as a zero and blended on mass.", "not blended, with the basis: not blended: no value for Egbema Medium.", "0.14 wt%, Obigbo Light''s own sulfur, as the only crude that carries a value.", "A refusal of the whole blend, since one property is missing."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A blank is absent. It is no zero, so the sulfur comes back not blended and the engine names Egbema Medium. 0.0888 is what a sulfur TYPED as 0 gives.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-blending-on-the-right-basis', 13, 'A laboratory sheet shows a sulfur of 0, and it is TYPED in for Egbema Medium. What comes back for the export blend?', '["not blended, with Egbema Medium named as missing.", "0.0888 wt%, with Egbema Medium named as missing.", "0.2642 wt%, with the zero skipped.", "0.0888 wt% on mass, with nothing named missing."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A typed 0 is a real zero, blended like any other figure: 0.0888 on mass, 0 properties listed as missing. A blank sulfur comes back not blended, with Egbema Medium named.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-blending-on-the-right-basis', 14, 'With Egbema Medium''s sulfur blank, what happens to the export blend''s API, TAN and viscosity?', '["They blend as usual: 32.8173, 0.3915 and 7.4743.", "They are withheld too, as incomplete.", "They blend on Obigbo Light alone, the crude with every value.", "API blends, and TAN and viscosity are withheld as mass-weighted."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Only the blank property is not blended. Every other property of the same blend is formed as usual: API 32.8173, TAN 0.3915 and viscosity 7.4743 read the same with the sulfur blank as with both sulfurs given.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-blending-on-the-right-basis', 15, 'A blank sulfur is reported as not blended, yet a crude with no API and no specific gravity is refused. Why the difference?', '["Gravity is a required field in the studio, and sulfur is an optional one.", "The engine refuses every blank, and sulfur is exempt as a per-mass property.", "Every property is weighted by density, so a missing gravity spoils the whole blend.", "Gravity blends on volume, and a volume property cannot be named as missing."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The refusal says it: No API or specific gravity for Egbema Medium. Every property here is weighted by density. A missing sulfur spoils one property, so only that one is not blended.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-viscosity-through-an-index', 1, 'Asarama Heavy and Ubie Condensate are blended 50 and 50 by volume. What viscosity does the engine give, and what does a straight average of the cSt figures on mass give?', '["339.5596 cSt from the engine, and 9.2496 from the straight average on mass.", "9.2496 cSt from the engine, and 330.3100 from the straight cSt average.", "6.8074 cSt from the engine, and 9.2496 from the straight average.", "9.2496 cSt from the engine, and 339.5596 from the straight average."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine gives 9.2496 through the Refutas index. The straight average on mass gives 339.5596, and linear average minus the engine prints 330.3100. 6.8074 is the index averaged on volume fractions.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-viscosity-through-an-index', 2, 'For the Obigbo export blend, 65 and 35, what does linear average minus the engine print for viscosity?', '["0.1636 cSt.", "3.4833 cSt.", "10.9576 cSt.", "330.3100 cSt."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Linear average minus the engine reads 3.4833 for the export blend. 0.1636 is the gap between the two index bases for the same blend, 10.9576 the linear average itself, and 330.3100 the gap for another pair.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-viscosity-through-an-index', 3, 'The engine''s index is VBI = A x ln(ln(nu + 0.8)) + B, nu in cSt. What values does it give for A and B?', '["14.5340 for A, and 10.9750 for B.", "10.9750 for A, and 14.5340 for B.", "141.5000 for A, and 131.5000 for B.", "14.5340 for A, and 0.8000 for B."]'::jsonb, 0, 'B is viscosityBlendIndex(e - 0.8), 10.9750, and A is viscosityBlendIndex(e^e - 0.8) minus B, 14.5340. 141.5000 and 131.5000 are the API constants, and 0.8000 is the offset inside the double log.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-viscosity-through-an-index', 4, 'How is the constant B read back from the engine itself?', '["As viscosityBlendIndex(e^e - 0.8), where the double log is 1.", "As viscosityFromBlendIndex of a very negative index.", "As viscosityBlendIndex(e - 0.8), where the double log is 0.", "As viscosityBlendIndex(1), the index of 1 cSt."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Where ln(nu + 0.8) is 1 the double log is 0, so the index there is B, 10.9750. At e^e - 0.8 the double log is 1, which gives A plus B. viscosityBlendIndex(1) is 3.2518.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-viscosity-through-an-index', 5, 'Asarama Heavy''s 610 cSt gives an index of 37.9879, and viscosityFromBlendIndex of that index returns 610.0000. What does this show?', '["The index is a straight line in viscosity above 100 cSt.", "The engine clamps a heavy crude to its typed viscosity.", "The two functions are exact inverses of each other.", "The index is blended on mass, since the trip is exact."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every crude''s viscosity goes into the index and comes back unchanged: 4.6 returns 4.6000, 22 returns 22.0000, 1.1 returns 1.1000.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-viscosity-through-an-index', 6, 'The domain probes run from 1 cSt to 1000 cSt. What does viscosityBlendIndex return at those two ends?', '["4.5307 at 1 cSt, and 37.9879 at 1000 cSt.", "23.5747 at 1 cSt, and 33.1962 at 1000 cSt.", "3.2518 at 1 cSt, and no index at 1000 cSt.", "3.2518 at 1 cSt, and 39.0657 at 1000 cSt."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The probes print 3.2518 at 1 cSt, 23.5747 at 10, 33.1962 at 100 and 39.0657 at 1000. 4.5307 is Ubie Condensate''s index at 1.1 cSt and 37.9879 is Asarama Heavy''s at 610 cSt. Of the probes, only 0.1 and 0.2 return no index.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-viscosity-through-an-index', 7, 'What basis does the engine name for a blend viscosity it returns?', '["Refutas index on volume fraction, the ASTM D7152 form.", "Refutas index on mass fraction.", "mass, the basis it names for sulfur and the metals.", "computed from the volume-blended specific gravity."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine names its basis in the result: "Refutas index on mass fraction". ASTM D7152 blends the same family of index on volume.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-viscosity-through-an-index', 8, 'What does the course say about the basis the Refutas index is averaged on?', '["The engine uses mass, ASTM D7152 uses volume, and the choice is held open.", "Mass is correct, and the ASTM D7152 volume figure is an error.", "Volume is correct, since ASTM D7152 is a standard practice for blend viscosity.", "The two bases give the same viscosity whenever the crudes differ in density."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Which basis to use is a HELD decision (FINDINGS C12). The course teaches what the engine does, what the other basis gives and that the two differ, and does not say which is right.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-viscosity-through-an-index', 9, 'For the Obigbo export blend, what does the index averaged on volume fractions give, beside the engine''s own figure?', '["7.4743 cSt, the same as on mass, since both crudes are light.", "7.3107 cSt, against the engine''s 7.4743 with the index on mass.", "10.9576 cSt, against the engine''s 7.4743 with the index on mass.", "0.1636 cSt, the viscosity the volume fractions leave."]'::jsonb, 1, 'On volume fractions the index gives 7.3107, the engine gives 7.4743 on mass, and mass basis minus volume basis prints 0.1636. 10.9576 is the straight cSt average on mass, with no index.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-viscosity-through-an-index', 10, 'Of the three blends printed on both bases, where is the mass basis minus the volume basis largest?', '["Asarama Heavy and Ubie Condensate, at 2.4422 cSt.", "Asarama Heavy and Ubie Condensate, at 330.3100 cSt.", "The Obigbo export blend, at 0.1636 cSt.", "Egbema Medium and Asarama Heavy, at 4.1448 cSt."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The difference column prints 0.1636, 2.4422 and 4.1448. 330.3100 is linear average minus the engine, a different comparison.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-viscosity-through-an-index', 11, 'What Refutas index does viscosityBlendIndex give Egbema Medium''s 22 cSt?', '["27.5437.", "23.5747.", "18.5704.", "37.9879."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Egbema Medium''s 22 cSt gives 27.5437, and viscosityFromBlendIndex of it returns 22.0000. 18.5704 is Obigbo Light''s index, 37.9879 is Asarama Heavy''s, and 23.5747 is the probe at 10 cSt.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-viscosity-through-an-index', 12, 'What does viscosityFromBlendIndex return for a very negative index?', '["0.8000 cSt, the offset inside the double log.", "0.0000 cSt, since the index runs down to nothing.", "0.2000 cSt, the lowest viscosity the index reaches.", "No viscosity, as for an index outside the domain."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Asked about a very negative index, viscosityFromBlendIndex returns 0.2000, the lowest viscosity the index reaches. The offset inside the double log, 0.8000, is 1 minus that figure.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-viscosity-through-an-index', 13, 'What does viscosityBlendIndex return for a viscosity of 0.2 cSt?', '["No index: 0.2 lies outside the domain.", "-122.8888, just inside the edge.", "0.2000, the lowest viscosity the index reaches.", "3.2518, used as a floor."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The probes print no index at 0.1 and at 0.2, and -122.8888 at 0.2001. The edge itself is outside the domain, and the lowest viscosity the index reaches is 0.2000.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-viscosity-through-an-index', 14, 'Why does the index domain end at 0.2000 cSt?', '["The engine clamps anything thinner than 0.2000 to 0.2001 before indexing.", "ln(nu + 0.8) is zero where nu + 0.8 is 1, and the double log is undefined there.", "Ubie Condensate at 1.1 cSt is the thinnest crude, and the edge sits below it.", "The index turns negative below 0.2000, and the engine refuses a negative index."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine reports the edge by asking its own inverse about a very negative index: the lowest viscosity the index reaches is 0.2000, and 1 minus that gives the offset 0.8000. The engine returns no index there and does not invent one. A negative index is allowed: 0.2001 gives -122.8888.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-viscosity-through-an-index', 15, 'One crude in a blend is typed at 0.1 cSt. What does blendCrudes return for the blend viscosity?', '["A refusal of the whole blend, as for a crude with no gravity.", "The blend viscosity with the 0.1 cSt clamped to 0.2001.", "The other crude''s viscosity alone, with the thin crude named.", "not blended, with the basis: not blended: a component viscosity is missing or outside the index domain."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The probe at 0.1 cSt returns no index. With one crude''s viscosity outside the domain, the blend''s viscosity is not blended, under that basis sentence, and the other properties of the blend are formed as usual. The engine returns no index there rather than a number.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-curve-and-its-cuts', 1, 'Obigbo Light''s curve carries the point 50 at 548. What does that point say?', '["Half the crude by mass has distilled by 548 F.", "50 percent of the crude boils in the band between 390 and 548 F.", "Half the crude by volume has distilled by 548 F.", "The crude''s midpoint cut, centred on 548 F, holds half the barrel."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A TBP point is cumulative: this volume percent has distilled by this temperature. The band from 390 to 548 F is the difference between the 30 and 50 percent points.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-curve-and-its-cuts', 2, 'What does volumePercentAt return on Obigbo Light at 300 F, and why?', '["20.2703, read on a straight line between 10 at 205 and 30 at 390.", "20.0000, the midpoint of the two neighbouring measured percentages.", "unknown, because 300 F is not a measured point.", "30.0000, the reading at the next measured point above 300 F."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine is linear between measured points. 300 F sits between 205 and 390 F, and the straight line there gives 20.2703. Only outside the measured range can a reading be unknown.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-curve-and-its-cuts', 3, 'What temperature does temperatureAtVolumePercent give for Obigbo Light at 25 percent?', '["unknown, because 25 percent was not measured on the curve.", "343.7500 F, linear between 10 at 205 and 30 at 390.", "205.0000 F, the measured point below, since the engine reads down.", "20.2703 F, the reading the curve gives at 300 F turned around."]'::jsonb, 1, 'temperatureAtVolumePercent is the inverse of volumePercentAt and is linear between points too. At 25 percent, between 10 and 30 percent, it gives 343.7500 F.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-curve-and-its-cuts', 4, 'The Ebocha partial assay is read at 240 F, between its points 4 at 110 and 25 at 370. What does the engine return?', '["unknown, since a partial curve is read at its measured points only.", "4.0000, its first measured point, held until the next one.", "14.5000, flagged as a partial reading so that no cut can use it.", "14.5000, the same straight-line reading a full curve gets."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine reads a curve linearly between measured points, and 240 F lies between Ebocha''s points at 110 and 370 F: 14.5000. Outside its measured range the partial curve reads unknown, as at 60 F and at 1000 F.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-curve-and-its-cuts', 5, 'volumePercentAt gives 100.0000 for Obigbo Light at 1500 F and unknown for Ebocha at 1000 F. Why the difference?', '["1500 F lies inside Obigbo Light''s measured range, and 1000 F lies outside Ebocha''s.", "The engine refuses every reading of a partial curve, inside its range or out.", "The engine extends Obigbo Light''s last segment and declines to extend Ebocha''s.", "Obigbo Light''s last point is at 100 percent; Ebocha''s stops at 88."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Above a last point at 100 percent everything has distilled, so the curve itself answers. Ebocha''s last point is 88 at 920, so above it the value is unknown. Obigbo Light''s range ends at 1380 F.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-curve-and-its-cuts', 6, 'At Ebocha''s first and last measured temperatures, which volume percents come back?', '["0.0000 at 110 F, and 100.0000 at 920 F.", "4.0000 at 110 F, and 88.0000 at 920 F.", "unknown at both, since each is an end of the range.", "0.0000 at 110 F, and 88.0000 at 920 F."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Both are measured points, 4 at 110 and 88 at 920, and the engine returns them: 4.0000 at 110 F and 88.0000 at 920 F. Outside them, at 60 F and at 1000 F, it returns unknown.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-curve-and-its-cuts', 7, 'What does temperatureAtVolumePercent return for Ebocha at 80 percent and at 95 percent?', '["848.8889 F at 80 percent, and unknown at 95 percent.", "848.8889 F at 80 percent, and 920.0000 F at 95 percent, its last point.", "unknown at both, since a partial curve has no inverse reading.", "848.8889 F at 80 percent, and 1220.0000 F at 95 percent."]'::jsonb, 0, '80 percent sits between the points 70 and 88, so it has a temperature. 95 percent lies past the last point at 88, so the temperature is unknown. 1220.0000 F is Obigbo Light''s reading at 95 percent.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-curve-and-its-cuts', 8, 'How does cutYields form each cut''s yield?', '["The share of measured points inside the cut''s band.", "The cut''s temperature span over the curve''s whole span.", "The curve at the cut''s upper bound minus the curve at its lower bound.", "The curve read once at the cut''s midpoint temperature."]'::jsonb, 2, 'cutYields returns each cut''s yield in volume percent of the whole crude: the curve at the cut''s upper bound minus the curve at its lower bound, each read linearly between measured points.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-curve-and-its-cuts', 9, 'Egbema Medium''s LPG / Light ends yield on the studio''s default cuts is 0.0000. What does that zero mean?', '["A blank: its curve does not reach down to 90 F, so the yield is printed as zero.", "A real answer: its curve starts 0 at 95, above the cut''s bound of 90 F.", "Its light ends are unmeasured, so the cut defaults to 0.0000.", "The cut is inverted for this crude and named in unknownCuts."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Below a first point at 0 percent the curve says nothing has distilled, so by 90 F nothing has. Obigbo Light, whose curve starts at 85 F, returns 0.4167 in the same cut.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-curve-and-its-cuts', 10, 'Among the four full crudes, which has the largest Vacuum residue yield on the default cuts?', '["Asarama Heavy: 30.0000 of the barrel.", "Egbema Medium: 23.1429 of the barrel, from its curve''s 70 at 880.", "Obigbo Light: 25.7110 of the barrel.", "Asarama Heavy: 35.5556."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The residue column prints 13.7736, 23.1429, 30.0000 and 0.0000. 35.5556 is Asarama Heavy''s Vacuum gasoil and 25.7110 Obigbo Light''s.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-curve-and-its-cuts', 11, 'A cut is drawn backwards on Obigbo Light, from 500 F to 350 F. What does cutYields return for it?', '["A negative yield: the Kerosene / Jet 18.2484 with its sign turned over.", "18.2484, the same band read the right way round.", "A refusal of the whole cut set, until the bounds are fixed.", "No yield; it is named in unknownCuts as inverted, 500 to 350 F."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine gives the inverted cut no yield and names it in unknownCuts (inverted, 500 to 350 F). 18.2484 is the Kerosene / Jet yield from 350 to 500 F.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-curve-and-its-cuts', 12, 'What does the Ebocha partial assay return on the studio''s default cuts?', '["Six yields scaled up to a total of 100.0000, and closes true.", "Two yields and a total of 33.6742, and closes true, since each cut the curve reaches has a yield.", "Two yields, Kerosene / Jet and Diesel / Gasoil, a total of 33.6742, and closes false.", "No yields at all, since a partial curve cannot be cut."]'::jsonb, 2, '4 of its 6 studio cuts reach outside the measured range and have no yield: LPG / Light ends, Naphtha, Vacuum gasoil and Vacuum residue. The known cuts are 16.3881 and 17.2861.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-curve-and-its-cuts', 13, 'Egbema Medium''s row of default cut yields: which figure is its Vacuum gasoil, 650 to 1000 F?', '["35.5556.", "23.1429.", "29.0000.", "17.8571."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Egbema Medium''s row reads 0.0000, 16.3636, 13.6364, 17.8571, 29.0000 and 23.1429 across the six default cuts, so its Vacuum gasoil is 29.0000. 35.5556 is Asarama Heavy''s Vacuum gasoil.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-curve-and-its-cuts', 14, 'A cut set drawn inside Ebocha''s curve, 110 to 370, 370 to 760 and 760 to 920 F, has every yield. Why does it still not close?', '["One of its three cuts reaches outside the curve and has no yield.", "Three cuts are too few; the engine closes only a full set of six.", "The cuts overlap at 370 and 760 F, and overlapping cuts cannot close.", "Its total is 84.0000, and closing needs a total within tolerance of 100 percent."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The inner set yields 21.0000, 45.0000 and 18.0000, with nothing unknown. It meets the first condition, every cut a yield, and misses the second, the total.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-curve-and-its-cuts', 15, 'The Vacuum residue cut has no upper bound. What does it take?', '["Everything not yet distilled at its lower bound of 1000 F.", "The volume between 1000 F and the curve''s last point, with the rest unknown.", "Nothing, since a cut with no upper bound has no yield to report.", "Whatever the other five cuts leave over."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A last cut with no upper bound runs to 100 percent. Asarama Heavy reads 70 at 1000, and its residue cut returns 30.0000; Ubie Condensate ends 100 at 760, and its residue is 0.0000.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-will-the-blend-stay-stable', 1, 'How does the engine form the colloidal instability index from a SARA analysis?', '["Aromatics plus resins, over saturates plus asphaltenes, so a high CII is stable.", "Saturates plus asphaltenes, over aromatics plus resins.", "Saturates plus aromatics over resins plus asphaltenes.", "Asphaltenes over resins."]'::jsonb, 1, 'CII = (saturates + asphaltenes) / (aromatics + resins). Saturates precipitate asphaltenes; aromatics and resins hold them. Below 0.7 the blend screens stable, and at or above 0.9 unstable.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-will-the-blend-stay-stable', 2, 'What does colloidalInstabilityIndex give for Ubie Condensate alone?', '["1.1598.", "0.7668.", "1.7614.", "8.3458."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The crude-alone table prints 1.1598 for Obigbo Light, 0.6026 for Egbema Medium, 0.7668 for Asarama Heavy and 8.3458 for Ubie Condensate. 1.7614 is Asarama Heavy with Ubie Condensate at 50 and 50.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-will-the-blend-stay-stable', 3, 'A blend''s CII is 0.7161. What does screenBlendStability return?', '["stable null in the uncertain band, with a spot test named.", "stable false, since 0.7161 is past the 0.7 threshold.", "stable true, since 0.7161 is below 0.9.", "stable false in the unstable band, with a lab test demanded."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Below 0.7 is stable (true), from 0.7 to below 0.9 is uncertain (null, spot test), and at or above 0.9 is unstable (false). Asarama Heavy with Egbema Medium at 70 and 30 lands at 0.7161.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-will-the-blend-stay-stable', 4, 'Which of these pairs screens stable on the CII, and at what index?', '["Asarama Heavy with Egbema Medium, 70 and 30, at 0.7161.", "Egbema Medium alone, at 0.6026, as the stabilising crude.", "Egbema Medium with Obigbo Light, 85 and 15, at 0.6634.", "The Obigbo export blend, 65 and 35, at 0.9163."]'::jsonb, 2, '0.6634 is below 0.7, so the pair returns stable true. 0.7161 is uncertain, the export blend''s 0.9163 is unstable, and 0.6026 is a crude alone, which is no blend verdict.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-will-the-blend-stay-stable', 5, 'Asarama Heavy with Egbema Medium at 70 and 30: what blended asphaltenes does the screen form on mass?', '["9.4791.", "11.9.", "6.7035.", "3.2450."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The pair''s row prints blended saturates 32.2493, aromatics 38.7563, resins 19.5153 and asphaltenes 9.4791, and its CII is 0.7161. 11.9 is Asarama Heavy''s own asphaltenes, 6.7035 belongs to the pair with Ubie Condensate and 3.2450 to Egbema Medium with Obigbo Light.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-will-the-blend-stay-stable', 6, 'Asarama Heavy alone has a CII of 0.7668 and Ubie Condensate alone 8.3458. How does the engine reach the 50 and 50 pair''s CII of 1.7614?', '["It averages the two crudes'' own CII figures, each weighted by the crude''s mass fraction in the blend.", "It averages the two crudes'' own CII figures on volume, since the pair is typed by volume in barrels.", "It blends the four SARA fractions on mass and forms the index from the blended fractions.", "It takes the larger of the two crudes'' own CII figures as the conservative screen."]'::jsonb, 2, 'screenBlendStability blends each SARA fraction on mass and forms CII = (saturates + asphaltenes) / (aromatics + resins) from the blended fractions: 57.0828, 24.7639, 11.4498 and 6.7035 give 1.7614.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-will-the-blend-stay-stable', 7, 'For Asarama Heavy with Ubie Condensate at 50 and 50, what CII does SARA blended on volume give, beside the engine''s figure?', '["1.7614 on volume; the engine''s figure with the SARA blended on mass is 1.9718.", "0.2104, the index the volume fractions give for the pair.", "1.7614 on both bases, since the pair is 50 and 50 by volume.", "1.9718 on volume; the engine''s mass figure is 1.7614."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine blends SARA on mass: 1.7614. SARA on volume gives 1.9718, and the volume reading minus the engine prints 0.2104. The engine never reports the volume figure.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-will-the-blend-stay-stable', 8, 'For Egbema Medium with Obigbo Light at 85 and 15, what does the CII from SARA on volume minus the engine''s CII print?', '["-0.0020.", "0.0037.", "0.2104.", "0.6671."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s CII is 0.6634 and SARA on volume gives 0.6671, and the difference column prints 0.0037. -0.0020 and 0.2104 are the other two pairs'' differences.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-will-the-blend-stay-stable', 9, 'With no SARA, the screen falls back to a gravity-contrast rule. What can that rule return?', '["stable false when it flags, and stable true whenever it does not.", "stable false when it flags, and stable null when it does not.", "stable true, null or false, the same three answers as the index.", "only stable null, since a rule of thumb decides nothing."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The rule can raise a flag and it cannot clear one: when it does not flag, the engine returns stable null. Stable true comes only from the CII route.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-will-the-blend-stay-stable', 10, 'The lighter crude is held at 40 API. At a contrast of 15.0000 the engine gives no verdict, and at 15.0100 it returns false. What do the two probes show?', '["The contrast must be above 15.0000; a contrast at the threshold does not flag.", "The flag is raised at a contrast of 15.0000 and above.", "The lighter crude, at 40 API, is below its own threshold.", "The rule reads contrasts only to two decimals near the edge."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Bisection on the engine puts the contrast threshold at 15.0000, with the lighter crude at 45 API. At exactly 15.0000 there is no verdict; at 15.0100 the rule flags. 40 API is past the lighter-crude threshold of 35.0000.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-will-the-blend-stay-stable', 11, 'A pair with no SARA has a lighter crude of 35 API and a heavier one of 19 API, a contrast of 16.0000. What does the rule return?', '["false: the contrast of 16.0000 is past its 15.0000 threshold, which is enough.", "false: both conditions are met, the contrast at 16.0000 and the lighter crude at 35.", "true: a contrast of 16.0000 with a lighter crude of 35 is a stable combination.", "No verdict: the lighter crude sits at its 35.0000 threshold and must be above it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A flag needs a contrast above one threshold and a lighter crude above the other, both at once. At 35.01 with the same heavier crude the rule flags, with a contrast of 16.0100.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-will-the-blend-stay-stable', 12, 'With SARA removed, the export blend''s gravity screen shows a contrast of 10.9000 and no verdict. With SARA, it screens unstable at 0.9163. What do the two answers show?', '["The index is in error, since the gravity screen found nothing wrong with the blend.", "The gravity rule is the stricter screen, since it withholds a verdict the index gives.", "The rule raised no flag and cannot clear one, and the index says unstable.", "A contrast of 10.9000 lies inside the range where the gravity rule raises its flag."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The gravity rule can raise a flag and it cannot clear one: when it does not flag, the engine returns stable null, never true. With the SARA taken away, the rule of thumb did not raise its flag, and the index says unstable.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-will-the-blend-stay-stable', 13, 'SARA is supplied for Obigbo Light only, in the export blend. What does the screen do?', '["It forms the CII from Obigbo Light''s SARA alone.", "It reads Egbema Medium''s missing SARA as zero and forms the CII.", "It refuses the blend until SARA is supplied for both.", "It falls back to api-contrast, and says SARA was not supplied for every crude."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The index needs SARA on every crude. With SARA on some crudes only, the basis is api-contrast, the stable answer is no verdict, and the message begins: SARA was not supplied for every crude, so this is an API-contrast screen only.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-will-the-blend-stay-stable', 14, 'screenBlendStability is called on its own with no SARA and one crude that has no API and no specific gravity. What does it return?', '["Basis none, the contrast not formed, and no verdict.", "Basis api-contrast, with the missing gravity taken as zero and the pair flagged.", "A refusal that names the crude with no gravity.", "Basis api-contrast, formed from the one crude that has a gravity."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Neither route is open, so no stability screen is made and the message says why. The refusal quoted is blendCrudes'', which refuses such a crude before any screen is made.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-will-the-blend-stay-stable', 15, 'Which route of the screen can return stable true?', '["Either route: an index below 0.7, or a gravity rule that does not flag.", "Only the CII route, with the blend''s index below 0.7.", "Only the gravity rule, when the contrast is at or below 15.0000.", "The CII route, with the blend''s index anywhere below 0.9."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Stable false comes from either route. No verdict comes from three places: the uncertain band, a gravity screen that did not flag, and a screen that could not be made. Stable true comes only from an index below 0.7.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 1, 'Which pairing of a figure and a basis is the engine''s own for the Obigbo export blend?', '["API 32.8173, on volume, like the specific gravity it came from", "sulfur 0.2642 wt%, on volume, the fraction the cargo is typed in", "nickel 6.5980 ppm, on the Refutas index on mass fraction", "viscosity 7.4743 cSt, on the Refutas index on mass fraction"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine names volume for specific gravity and mass for sulfur, TAN, nitrogen, nickel and vanadium. Viscosity carries "Refutas index on mass fraction", and API carries its own sentence: "computed from the volume-blended specific gravity, never averaged directly".'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 2, 'What CII and verdict does the export blend carry with every SARA supplied?', '["0.9163, band unstable, stable false.", "0.9163, band uncertain.", "0.6634, band stable, stable true.", "1.1598, which is Obigbo Light''s own index alone."]'::jsonb, 0, 'At or above 0.9 the band is unstable, and 0.9163 is at or above it. 0.6634 is Egbema Medium with Obigbo Light at 85 and 15, and 1.1598 is Obigbo Light alone.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 3, 'Every per-mass property of the export blend is weighted by which fractions?', '["0.6500 and 0.3500, the volume shares as typed.", "0.4775 and 0.3063, the mass fractions the blend carries.", "0.6346 for Obigbo Light and 0.3654 for Egbema Medium.", "0.6346 for sulfur, and 0.6500 for the two metals, nickel and vanadium."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine forms the mass fractions once, from the volume shares and each crude''s specific gravity, and every per-mass property reads them. 0.4775 and 0.3063 belong to the three-crude blend.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 4, 'Where does Ubie Condensate''s TBP curve stop?', '["90 percent at 600 F.", "100 percent at 760 F.", "100 percent at 1380 F.", "88 percent at 920 F."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Ubie Condensate''s curve runs from 0 at 70 to 100 at 760. 90 at 600 is its point before the last, 100 at 1380 is Obigbo Light''s end point and 88 at 920 is the Ebocha partial assay''s last point.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 5, 'On the studio''s default cuts, which of the export blend''s cuts have no yield, and does its set close?', '["LPG / Light ends, since Egbema Medium''s curve starts at 95 F; it does not close.", "None; the six yields total 100.0000 and the set closes.", "None; the yields are normalised to 100.0000 before the set closes.", "Vacuum residue, which has no upper bound; it does not close."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The export blend''s yields are 0.2708, 22.1456, 16.6342, 17.0344, 26.8621 and 17.0528, total 100.0000. Closes: true. Cuts with no yield: nothing. A total is reported as it computes and never normalised.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 6, 'Which library crude has the largest Naphtha yield on the studio''s default cuts?', '["Obigbo Light, at 25.2590, the light crude.", "Ubie Condensate, at 22.7807 volume percent.", "Ubie Condensate, at 52.5974.", "Egbema Medium, at 16.3636, the medium crude."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Ubie Condensate''s Naphtha cut is 52.5974, the largest in the Naphtha column; 22.7807 is its Kerosene / Jet, 25.2590 is Obigbo Light''s Naphtha and 16.3636 Egbema Medium''s.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 7, 'In the three-crude blend Asarama Heavy is 20 by volume. What is its mass fraction, and what is the vanadium gap there?', '["0.2162, and vanadium on mass minus volume is 1.5761 ppm.", "0.2000, since a volume share carries straight over, and 0.1123 ppm.", "0.1846, and 1.5761 ppm.", "0.2162, and vanadium on mass minus volume is 0.6702 ppm there."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Asarama Heavy, at 0.9516, carries 0.2162 of the mass. 0.1846 is its volume fraction when 20 is typed by mass, 0.6702 is the nickel gap, and 0.1123 is the export blend''s vanadium gap.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 8, 'What can the Ebocha partial assay blend for?', '["Every property, with its blanks read as zero.", "Nothing, since a partial assay is refused.", "Gravity alone, since its curve stops at 88.", "Gravity and sulfur; any other property is not blended, with Ebocha named."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Ebocha carries an API of 31.4 and a sulfur of 0.22, and everything else is not given. It can blend for those two; for any property it does not carry, the engine names it each time.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 9, 'Which library crude carries the most asphaltenes in its SARA analysis?', '["Egbema Medium, at 3.5 wt%.", "Obigbo Light, at 1.7 wt%.", "Asarama Heavy, at 11.9 wt%.", "Ubie Condensate, at 89.1 wt%."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The asphaltenes column reads 1.7, 3.5, 11.9 and 0.2 wt%. 89.1 is Ubie Condensate''s saturates.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 10, 'The export blend''s result lists specific gravity beside a basis. Which pair is it?', '["0.8611, on volume.", "0.8611, on mass.", "0.8804, on volume.", "0.8408, on volume."]'::jsonb, 0, 'blendCrudes blends specific gravity on volume and names that basis: 0.8611. 0.8804 is the three-crude blend''s specific gravity and 0.8408 is Obigbo Light''s own.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 11, 'The export blend''s own curve gives it six cut yields. Which is the one for Vacuum residue?', '["17.0344.", "13.7736.", "23.1429.", "17.0528."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The blend''s residue yield is 17.0528. 17.0344 is its Diesel / Gasoil, and 13.7736 and 23.1429 are Obigbo Light''s and Egbema Medium''s own residue yields.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 12, 'Why does the gravity rule flag Asarama Heavy with Ubie Condensate when SARA is left out?', '["Its CII of 1.7614 is past the 0.9 edge, which the rule reads.", "A contrast of 37.4000 with a light paraffinic crude, past both thresholds.", "A contrast of 37.4000 on its own; the lighter crude is not checked.", "Asarama Heavy''s 11.9 wt% asphaltenes, which the rule reads directly."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A flag needs a contrast above 15.0000 and a lighter crude above 35.0000, both at once. Ubie Condensate is 54.6 API. The rule reads gravities only; the CII route is a different screen.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 13, 'Which figures does the library print for Obigbo Light''s API and sulfur?', '["36.8 API and 0.14 wt% sulfur.", "0.8408 API and 0.14 wt% sulfur.", "36.8 API, 0.48 wt% sulfur.", "54.6 API, 0.03 wt% sulfur."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Obigbo Light is 36.8 API, specific gravity 0.8408, with 0.14 wt% sulfur. 0.48 is Egbema Medium''s sulfur, and 54.6 and 0.03 are Ubie Condensate''s.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 14, 'The export blend''s 7.4743 cSt is reported under a held convention. What does that mean for its reader?', '["It is provisional, and the engine will recompute it on volume fractions when asked.", "It is the correct figure, and the volume-basis 7.3107 is a slip.", "It is the engine''s figure on the basis it names, and the choice of basis is held open.", "It is a straight cSt average on mass, standing in until a basis is decided."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine blends the Refutas index on mass fraction and names that basis. ASTM D7152 blends on volume, which gives 7.3107 for this blend, and the course does not say which is right.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 15, 'A buyer reads the export blend''s gravity, sulfur and viscosity. Which set is the engine''s result?', '["32.9850 API, 0.2590 wt% sulfur and 7.3107 cSt.", "32.8173 API, 0.2590 wt% sulfur and 10.9576 cSt on mass.", "32.9850 API, 0.2642 wt% and 7.4743 cSt.", "32.8173 API, 0.2642 wt% sulfur and 7.4743 cSt."]'::jsonb, 3, '32.9850 is the volume-weighted mean of the API numbers, 0.2590 is sulfur on volume, 7.3107 is the index on volume and 10.9576 is the straight cSt average. None of them is in the engine''s result.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 1, 'On every blend in the gravity table, blend API differs from the volume-weighted mean of the API numbers. What lies behind that?', '["The engine takes a shrinkage allowance off the blend when crudes of different gravity mix.", "Volume fractions give the lighter crude too little weight, so the engine adds a correction.", "The engine averages API on mass fractions, which drift from the typed volume shares.", "API is a hyperbola in specific gravity, and specific gravity is what blends on volume."]'::jsonb, 3, 'API = A / SG - B is a hyperbola in specific gravity, and blendCrudes blends specific gravity on volume, taking volume as conserved; API itself is never averaged. The volume-weighted mean misses on every row: -0.6192, -0.1677, -2.0889 and -0.3860.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 2, 'For three crudes at 50, 30 and 20, the digest prints the figure 29.6100 in two places. What does it stand for in each?', '["The engine''s API for the blend typed by volume, and the mass-weighted mean of the API numbers for the same blend.", "The engine''s API for the blend typed by mass, and a rounding of the 29.2240 typed by volume.", "The volume-weighted mean of the API numbers, printed a second time as a check on the engine.", "The volume-weighted mean of the API numbers for the blend typed by volume, and the engine''s API for the blend typed by mass."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Typed by volume the engine gives 29.2240 and the shortcut gives 29.6100. Typed by mass the engine gives 29.6100. The same three numbers make two different blends depending on their basis.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 3, 'What does the engine assume about volume when two crudes of different gravity are poured together?', '["The volume shrinks in proportion to the two crudes'' API contrast on mixing.", "Mass is conserved, and the volume is found from the blend''s API.", "Volume is taken as conserved, as mass is.", "Volume is conserved only within 15.0000 API of contrast."]'::jsonb, 2, 'blendCrudes blends specific gravity on volume, and the digest states the assumption: mass is conserved and volume is taken as conserved.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 4, 'A recipe types a share of -10 for one of two crudes. What does blendCrudes return?', '["The blend, with the negative share read as zero and normalised.", "REFUSED: A blend share must be a number of zero or more.", "not blended, with that crude named as missing.", "REFUSED: The blend shares add up to zero."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A share of -10 gets its own refusal: A blend share must be a number of zero or more.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 5, 'Shares of 0 and 0 are typed for a two-crude blend. What does the engine return?', '["not blended, with both crudes named as missing.", "REFUSED: A blend share must be a number of zero or more.", "A blend of equal parts, since equal shares normalise to 50 and 50.", "REFUSED: The blend shares add up to zero."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Each zero share is a number of zero or more, and the pair is refused on its total: The blend shares add up to zero.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 6, 'Egbema Medium arrives with neither API nor specific gravity, and no crude has SARA. Compare the blend engine and the stand-alone stability screen.', '["Both refuse, in the same words, naming the crude that has no gravity.", "The blend engine stops with a refusal; the stand-alone screen makes no screen and gives no verdict.", "blendCrudes names the crude missing and blends the rest; the screen returns api-contrast.", "blendCrudes refuses; the screen reads the missing gravity as zero and raises its flag."]'::jsonb, 1, 'blendCrudes refuses: every property here is weighted by density. Called outside blendCrudes, screenBlendStability reports basis none, because neither the index route nor the gravity route is open.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 7, 'Two blends lose their viscosity for different reasons: in one a component''s cSt is blank, in the other it is typed as 0.1. How does the engine report each?', '["not blended in both, under one basis sentence for missing or outside the domain.", "not blended for the blank, and 0.2000 cSt for the 0.1, clamped to the edge.", "A refusal for the blank, and not blended for the 0.1 outside the domain.", "not blended for the blank, and -122.8888 for the 0.1 near the edge."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The basis reads: not blended: a component viscosity is missing or outside the index domain. -122.8888 is the index at 0.2001, inside the edge, and 0.2000 is the lowest viscosity the index reaches.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 8, 'Which of these is formed on MASS fractions in the engine''s result for a blend?', '["The blend''s specific gravity, which API is converted from.", "The sulfur shortcut printed beside the engine''s figure.", "The SARA fractions behind the blend''s CII.", "The cut yields read off the curve."]'::jsonb, 2, 'screenBlendStability blends each SARA fraction on mass. Specific gravity blends on volume, the shortcut column weights sulfur by volume fraction, and yields are on volume off the curve.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 9, 'The engine puts a half-and-half mix of Egbema Medium with Asarama Heavy at 91.4520 cSt. Which printed gap measures how far a plain linear cSt average overshoots that?', '["4.1448 cSt, the gap between the two index bases.", "87.3072 cSt.", "232.9041 cSt.", "330.3100 cSt, the gap for the heavy and condensate pair."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The straight average on mass is 324.3561, and linear average minus the engine prints 232.9041. 4.1448 is mass basis minus volume basis for the index, 87.3072 is the index on volume, and 330.3100 belongs to Asarama Heavy with Ubie Condensate.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 10, 'Ubie Condensate is the thinnest crude in the library, at 1.1 cSt. Where does it sit against the Refutas index domain?', '["Outside it, since the index is undefined below 1 cSt and a condensate is thinner.", "Inside it, with an index of 4.5307; the edge is at 0.2000 cSt.", "At the edge itself, where the index reads -122.8888.", "Inside it, with an index of 3.2518 read at 1 cSt."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every crude in the library sits inside the domain. The index is undefined only where ln(nu + 0.8) is zero or less, and the lowest viscosity it reaches is 0.2000. 3.2518 is the index of 1 cSt.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 11, 'At 60 F, one library curve reads 0.0000 and another reads unknown: Obigbo Light and the Ebocha partial assay. What decides which answer each gets?', '["Obigbo Light is the lighter crude, so less of it has distilled by 60 F.", "The engine needs seven measured points to read below a curve''s first one.", "Only Obigbo Light''s curve begins at 0 percent, which says nothing has distilled below 85 F.", "The engine clamps a full curve to its first reading and leaves a partial one blank, as unknown."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A curve answers outside its range only where it says so itself. Ebocha''s curve starts at 4 at 110, so below 110 F the value is unknown.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 12, 'Obigbo Light is 95 percent distilled at which temperature?', '["1220.0000 F, between 90 at 1060 and 100 at 1380.", "unknown, since 95 percent lies past its 90 percent point.", "1380.0000 F, the curve''s end point.", "1060 F, the last point below 95 percent, held flat."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Obigbo Light''s curve has a point at 100 percent, so 95 percent lies between measured points and is read on the straight line: 1220.0000 F. Ebocha, which stops at 88, returns unknown at 95 percent.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 13, 'Which library crude brings the most Diesel / Gasoil, the 500 to 650 F cut, per barrel?', '["Obigbo Light, at 16.5914, the light crude of the library.", "Egbema Medium, at 17.8571.", "Ubie Condensate, at 14.8897.", "Asarama Heavy, at 35.5556, the heaviest of the four."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Diesel / Gasoil column, 500 to 650 F, prints 16.5914, 17.8571, 14.0097 and 14.8897 for the four full crudes, and Ebocha''s is 17.2861. 35.5556 is Asarama Heavy''s Vacuum gasoil, 650 to 1000 F.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 14, 'Ubie Condensate''s Vacuum residue yield is 0.0000. Is that zero a blank?', '["Yes: its curve stops short of 1000 F, so the cut is unknown and prints zero.", "Yes: a condensate carries no residue analysis, so the cut defaults to zero.", "No: the residue cut is left out of any set whose total passes 100.0000.", "No: its curve ends 100 at 760, so everything has distilled by 1000 F."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Above a last point at 100 percent everything has distilled, so the residue cut, which takes everything not yet distilled at 1000 F, is a real 0.0000. The set closes at 100.0000 with nothing unknown.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 15, 'Obigbo Light''s curve runs from 0 at 85 to 100 at 1380. What does temperatureAtVolumePercent return at 0 percent and at 100 percent?', '["85.0000 F and 1380.0000 F.", "0.0000 F and 100.0000 F.", "unknown at both ends of the curve.", "85.0000 F, and unknown at 100 percent."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Both are measured points, and the engine returns 85.0000 F at 0 percent and 1380.0000 F at 100 percent. A reading is unknown only outside a curve''s points, as Ebocha''s is at 2 and at 95 percent.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 16, 'What are the two thresholds of the gravity-contrast rule, as bisection on the engine finds them?', '["An API contrast of 15.0000 and a lighter crude API of 35.0000.", "0.7 and 0.9, the two edges held in CII_BANDS and read against the index.", "A contrast of 15.0100 and a lighter crude of 35.01 API.", "A contrast of 37.4000 and a contrast of 10.9000."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The two thresholds are not exported, so the engine is probed. A flag needs a contrast above 15.0000 and a lighter crude above 35.0000, both at once. 0.7 and 0.9 are the CII band edges.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 17, 'Asarama Heavy with Egbema Medium at 70 and 30 has a CII of 0.7161. What does the engine''s message tell the terminal to do?', '["Do not commingle without a lab test.", "Supply SARA for a colloidal instability index.", "Spot test to ASTM D7112 or D7157 before commingling.", "Nothing more; the asphaltenes are held by the aromatics and resins."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The uncertain message reads: Uncertain. The index sits in the band where blends go either way; spot test to ASTM D7112 or D7157 before commingling.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 18, 'Obigbo Light is typed at 50 by volume beside Egbema Medium at 30 and Asarama Heavy at 20. What is its mass fraction?', '["0.5000.", "0.5223.", "0.4775.", "0.6346."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Typed at 50 by volume, Obigbo Light''s mass fraction is 0.4775, and mass fraction minus volume fraction prints -0.0225. 0.5000 is its volume fraction here, 0.5223 its volume fraction when 50 is typed by mass, and 0.6346 its mass fraction in the export blend.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 19, 'The export blend''s CII is 0.9163. Which band edge decides its answer?', '["0.9: at or above it the answer is unstable.", "0.7: past it the answer is uncertain.", "0.9: the answer stays uncertain until the index passes it.", "No edge: the gravity screen decides for the export blend."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Below 0.7 is stable, from 0.7 to below 0.9 is uncertain, and at or above 0.9 is unstable. 0.9163 returns stable false, band unstable.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 20, 'Obigbo Light''s CII alone is 1.1598, yet Egbema Medium with Obigbo Light at 85 and 15 screens stable. How can that be?', '["An index past 0.9 is set aside when its crude is under half of the blend.", "The engine averages the two crudes'' own CII on volume, which lands under 0.7.", "The pair''s index is formed from the pair''s SARA, blended on mass.", "Obigbo Light''s SARA is dropped from the blend, because its own index is already past 0.9 on its own."]'::jsonb, 2, 'screenBlendStability blends the four SARA fractions on mass and forms the CII from them: 36.6357, 41.3109, 18.8084 and 3.2450 give 0.6634, stable true. Obigbo Light''s 1.1598 is the index of that crude alone.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 21, 'In the three-crude blend at 50, 30 and 20 by volume, sulfur on mass is 0.6138 wt%. What is 0.5840?', '["The engine''s sulfur for the blend typed by volume, once the mass fractions from resolveFractions are applied.", "Two things: this blend''s volume shortcut, and the engine''s figure when 50, 30 and 20 are mass shares.", "Sulfur on volume for the export blend of Obigbo Light and Egbema Medium, printed beside this one for comparison.", "The mass minus volume gap for sulfur in this blend, carried into the next row."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The per-mass table prints 0.5840 as sulfur on volume for the blend typed by volume, and the side-by-side table prints 0.5840 as the sulfur of the blend typed by mass. The gap for this blend is 0.0298.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 22, 'The gravity fallback is run on the heavy crude and the condensate with no SARA. Which contrast does its row print?', '["10.9000.", "37.4000.", "16.0100.", "15.0000."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The gravity-screen row for that pair prints basis api-contrast, contrast 37.4000 and stable false. 10.9000 is the export blend''s contrast, 15.0000 the contrast threshold and 16.0100 a probe.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 23, 'Inside blendCrudes, which function turns the typed volume shares into mass shares?', '["resolveFractions.", "blendOnMass.", "sgFromApi.", "screenBlendStability."]'::jsonb, 0, 'resolveFractions forms each crude''s mass share once: volume share times specific gravity, over the sum of those products. The other three are crudeAssay functions with other jobs.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 24, 'Which exported table holds the bands the stability screen reads its index against?', '["CII_BANDS, in crudeAssay.", "SPEC_TEMPLATES, in productBlending.", "LP_STATUS, in lib/lp/simplex.", "BLEND_BASIS, in productBlending."]'::jsonb, 0, 'crudeAssay exports 18 functions and 1 table, CII_BANDS. The engines state CII_BANDS.STABLE as 0.7 and CII_BANDS.UNSTABLE as 0.9, the two band edges.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 25, 'Where does the 0.8408 that Obigbo Light carries in the mass-share table come from?', '["A density measured in a laboratory and typed in.", "The blend''s 0.8611 scaled by the crude''s share.", "apiFromSg, run on the crude''s blend share.", "sgFromApi applied to its typed 36.8 API."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Specific gravity is the one library column the engine computes, from API through sgFromApi. The mass-share conversion then uses it: volume share times specific gravity, over the sum of those products.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 26, 'Egbema Medium with Asarama Heavy, half and half by volume: which printed figure is the viscosity with the index averaged on volume fractions?', '["91.4520 cSt, the figure the engine reports.", "87.3072 cSt.", "4.1448 cSt, the gap between the two bases.", "324.3561 cSt, a straight cSt average on mass."]'::jsonb, 1, 'This pair prints 91.4520 with the index on mass, which is the engine''s, and 87.3072 on volume. The course holds the choice of basis open, and the two are read side by side.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 27, 'What does viscosityBlendIndex return just inside the domain edge, at 0.2001 cSt?', '["-122.8888.", "no index, since 0.2001 still lies outside the domain.", "0.2000, the lowest viscosity the index reaches.", "3.2518, the same as the index of 1 cSt."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The probe table puts -122.8888 at 0.2001 cSt, inside the domain; the rows at 0.1 and 0.2 read no index.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 28, 'Why do Ebocha''s Kerosene / Jet and Diesel / Gasoil cuts have yields when its other four default cuts do not?', '["They are the cuts the partial assay was run for, and the rest were skipped.", "Their bounds, 350 to 650 F, lie inside its measured range of 110 to 920 F.", "The engine extends the curve for the middle cuts and for no others.", "Their yields are the 33.6742 total, shared between the two by span."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A cut has a yield only when the curve can answer at both bounds. Ebocha''s other four cuts reach below 110 F or above 920 F, where its curve is unknown. The two known cuts are 16.3881 and 17.2861.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 29, 'Ebocha''s inner cut set totals 84.0000 with no cut unknown; its default set totals 33.6742 with four unknown. Which closing condition does each fail?', '["The inner set fails on an unknown cut; the default set fails on its total alone.", "Both fail on the total alone, since unknown cuts do not count against closing.", "The inner set closes; only the default set fails.", "The inner set fails on its total alone; the default set fails on both."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A set closes only when every cut has a yield and the total is within tolerance of 100 percent. The inner set has every yield and misses the total; the default set misses both.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 30, 'Ebocha''s curve is asked for the temperature where 2 percent has come over. What comes back?', '["110.0000 F, held at its first point.", "0.0000 F, since nothing has distilled.", "unknown.", "4.0000 F, its first measured percent."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Ebocha''s first point is 4 at 110, and its curve does not start at 0 percent, so below 4 percent the temperature is unknown. At 4 percent it returns 110.0000 F.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 31, 'When the gravity rule does not flag the export blend, what does the engine''s message say about the result?', '["That the blend screens stable on gravity contrast, pending a SARA analysis.", "That the blend is uncertain and needs a spot test to ASTM D7112 or D7157.", "That the spread is not the classic combination, which is no evidence the blend is stable.", "That no stability screen was made because not every crude has a gravity."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The message reads, in part: The gravity spread is not the classic heavy-plus-light-paraffinic combination, which is not evidence that the blend is stable. Supply SARA for a real index.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 32, 'Which basis does the export blend''s screen carry when its SARA is removed?', '["api-contrast, with a contrast of 10.9000.", "cii, band unstable, at 0.9163.", "none, with the contrast not formed.", "api-contrast, at a contrast of 37.4000 that flags."]'::jsonb, 0, 'With no SARA and both gravities present, the screen falls back to the API contrast, 10.9000, and gives no verdict. 37.4000 is Asarama Heavy with Ubie Condensate, which flags.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 33, 'Which of these is the export blend''s nickel as the engine reports it?', '["6.5980 ppm, on mass.", "6.4550, on volume.", "0.1430 ppm, mass minus volume.", "14.2202 ppm, on mass, for three crudes."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Nickel is per unit mass, so the engine gives 6.5980 on mass. 6.4550 is the volume shortcut, 0.1430 is mass minus volume, and 14.2202 is the three-crude blend''s nickel.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 34, 'Why does the digest print a column called the volume-weighted mean of the API numbers when the engine never reports it?', '["Because the engine reports it as a check figure whenever the blend API is formed.", "Because contracts quote it, and the engine converts it to specific gravity later.", "So the shortcut can be read beside the answer, computed with the engine''s own blendOnVolume.", "Because it is the engine''s answer for a blend whose shares are typed by volume."]'::jsonb, 2, 'API itself is never averaged. The column is the shortcut the engine refuses to take, computed so that it can be read beside the right answer.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 35, 'Which function does the studio name for what a crude is worth against the crude already bought?', '["cutYields, on the crude''s own curve.", "netbackValue, with its marker differential.", "blendCrudes, with its mass fractions.", "colloidalInstabilityIndex, on the blend."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The function table pairs the value question with netbackValue, with its marker differential. cutYields answers what the barrel turns into, and blendCrudes what happens to the properties when two crudes mix.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 36, 'Where does the 0.8000 inside the Refutas index come from, in the engine''s own reading?', '["viscosityBlendIndex(e - 0.8), which gives the constant B.", "The lowest viscosity the index reaches, read from the inverse.", "A minus B, read from the two constants the engine gives.", "1 minus viscosityFromBlendIndex of a very negative index."]'::jsonb, 3, 'viscosityFromBlendIndex of a very negative index is 0.2000, the lowest viscosity the index reaches, and 1 minus that gives the offset 0.8000. viscosityBlendIndex(e - 0.8) gives B, 10.9750.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 37, 'How much of Obigbo Light has distilled by 600 F, as the engine reads it?', '["50.0000, held at the point below.", "55.3608, linear between 50 at 548 and 70 at 742.", "unknown, since 600 F is not a measured point.", "20.2703, the reading between 10 and 30 percent."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Between measured points the engine reads a straight line. 20.2703 is the reading at 300 F, between 205 and 390 F.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 38, 'Which probe raises the gravity rule''s flag?', '["40 and 25 API, a contrast of 15.0000.", "35 and 19 API, a contrast of 16.0000.", "The export blend, at a contrast of 10.9000.", "40 and 24.99 API, a contrast of 15.0100."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The contrast must be above 15.0000 and the lighter crude above 35.0000. 40 and 25 sits at the contrast threshold, 35 and 19 at the lighter-crude threshold, and both return no verdict.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 39, 'What API does apiFromSg give a specific gravity of 0.85?', '["45.3750.", "25.7222.", "36.8000.", "34.9706."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The equal-steps table prints 57.1667 at 0.75, 45.3750 at 0.8, 34.9706 at 0.85 and 25.7222 at 0.9. 36.8000 is Obigbo Light''s API returned by the round trip.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 40, 'Why can the engine average specific gravity on a straight line and still refuse to average cSt figures that way?', '["Specific gravity blends on volume, and viscosity mixes nowhere near linearly.", "Specific gravity is per unit mass, and viscosity is per unit volume.", "cSt figures carry one decimal as typed, and averaging them loses precision.", "Viscosity is measured on a different temperature scale from specific gravity."]'::jsonb, 0, 'blendCrudes blends specific gravity on volume, taking volume as conserved. Viscosity mixes nowhere near linearly, so it is blended through the Refutas index and inverted.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 41, 'Where on the scale does one equal move in specific gravity shift the API furthest?', '["From 0.95 to 1, a step of -7.4474.", "From 0.75 to 0.8, a step of -11.7917.", "From 0.85 to 0.9, a step of -9.2484.", "Every step, by the same -10.4044."]'::jsonb, 1, '-11.7917 between 0.75 and 0.8 is the largest step in the table, and -7.4474 between 0.95 and 1 the smallest. Equal steps of specific gravity are unequal steps of API.'),
  ('crude', 'beginner', 'final', null, 42, 'Asarama Heavy''s curve reads 70 at 1000. What is its Vacuum residue yield on the default cuts?', '["35.5556, its Vacuum gasoil.", "unknown, with no upper bound.", "70 percent, the curve at 1000 F.", "30.0000."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The residue cut has no upper bound, so it takes everything not yet distilled at 1000 F: 30.0000. 35.5556 is Asarama Heavy''s Vacuum gasoil, 650 to 1000 F.');
